From: ral at lcfltd.com (Robert A LaBudde)
Date: Thu, 04 Mar 2010 14:27:59 -0500
Subject: Matrix Algebra Review for Statisticians course at statistics.com April 16, 2010
References: <4B8FDDD9.2020709@dataanalyticscorp.com>
Message-ID: <0KYR008K6VF2F7Y7@vms173001.mailsrvcs.net>

Those drawn more to the data side of statistics than the math side 
often shut their eyes when matrix algebra rears its head. If you're 
one of those people, and would like a bit of background before 
tackling the many topics in multivariate statistics that involve some 
matrix algebra, our new 'Matrix Algebra' review course with Robert 
LaBudde (April 16 - May 7) is for you.

Statistics deals with collections of data organized in 1, 2, 3 or 
more dimensions. Compactly representing such data is best 
accomplished by the use of matrix notation, particularly when 
solutions to optimization (e.g., regression) or estimating (i.e, 
models) are involved.

'Matrix Algebra,' a 3-week course starting April 16, will provide the 
basics of vector and matrix algebra and operations necessary to 
understand multivariate statistical methods, including the notions of 
the matrix inverse, generalized inverse and eigenvalues and 
eigenvectors. After successfully completing this course, you will be 
able to use and understand vector and matrix operations and 
equations, find and use a matrix inverse, and use and understand the 
eigenset of a symmetric matrix. Example problems and applications to 
statistics are given in S/R.

Robert LaBudde is president of Least Cost Formulations, Ltd., a 
mathematical software development company specializing in 
optimization and process control software for manufacturing 
companies. He is the co-author with Dr. Michael Chernick of the 
forthcoming book "An Introduction to Bootstrap Methods with 
Applications to R," and has served on the faculties of the University 
of Wisconsin, Massachusetts Institute of Technology, Old Dominion 
University and North Carolina State University.

The course requires about 15 hours a week and there are no set hours 
when you must be available.

Register here:

http://www.statistics.com/ourcourses/matrixalgebra

